#Once the Agent Started Playing, Then the Problems Started

Once the first version of the agent could actually play the game, I expected the next step to be relatively straightforward: run it, observe the results, and gradually improve the strategy.

Instead, this was where the project started becoming much more iterative.

The basic loop worked. The agent could ask questions, receive answers, update its search space, and eventually make a guess.

But as soon as I started watching actual games, I began noticing small problems in its behaviour.

Some were obvious bugs.

Others were consequences of decisions I had made earlier without fully thinking through how they would behave over multiple turns.

The development process became a repeated cycle:

Build an idea
      ↓
Run the agent
      ↓
Notice strange behaviour
      ↓
Ask why it happened
      ↓
Change the logic
      ↓
Run it again
      ↓
Discover the next problem

This was probably the first point where the project started teaching me more than simply implementing an algorithm would have.

The First Problem: Binary Features

One of the first issues appeared with binary features.

Consider a feature like:

Sex = male / female

The agent could ask:

Is Sex = male?

and receive:

No

At that point, if those were the only two possible values, the agent already had all the information available from that feature.

The answer logically implied:

Sex = female

But the agent did not initially understand that.

It could later waste another turn asking:

Is Sex = female?

This was one of those problems that seemed obvious once I saw it happen.

The search space had already encoded the answer, but the agent's questioning logic did not treat the entire feature as resolved.

The Fix

I changed the update logic so that binary features could be resolved after a single answer.

If the agent received:

Is Sex = male?
→ No

the remaining value was effectively known.

The feature could then be marked as resolved and excluded from future questions.

This introduced a broader idea into the agent:

A question does not only provide information about the exact value that was asked. Sometimes the structure of the feature itself allows additional information to be deduced.

This was the first time I started thinking about the difference between tracking individual questions and tracking what the agent had actually learned.

The Entropy Problem: Why Was the Agent Stuck on One Feature?

After fixing binary redundancy, another behaviour became obvious.

The agent sometimes kept returning to the same feature.

For example, it could repeatedly focus on Sex while ignoring other potentially useful features such as:

Region
Age
Hair color
Industry

The immediate reason was related to how I was using entropy for feature selection.

At every turn, the agent recalculated feature scores from the current search space and selected the best-scoring feature.

But nothing initially told it:

You have already learned everything useful from this feature.

So even though the search space had changed, the feature-selection process could continue evaluating features that should effectively have been removed from consideration.

Adding Feature Resolution

I introduced an asked_features tracker.

The name is slightly misleading in retrospect, because the purpose was not simply to remember every feature the agent had touched.

The goal was to track whether a feature had become resolved.

A feature would be excluded when:

it was logically resolved, as with a binary feature;
only one possible value remained;
asking another question about it could no longer provide useful information.

This changed the selection process from:

Evaluate every feature to:

Evaluate every unresolved feature

That distinction mattered.

The agent was no longer simply calculating which feature had the highest score.

It first had to decide which parts of the state still contained uncertainty worth investigating.

Guessing Too Early

Another issue appeared when the search space became small.

I initially allowed the agent to make guesses before the candidate pool had been reduced to a single character.

In practice, I noticed behaviour I did not like.

The agent could reach two remaining candidates and immediately guess one, even though it might still have enough information available to distinguish them.

This made me reconsider what the probabilistic guessing mechanism was actually supposed to do.

There was a difference between:

The agent has enough information to guess.

and:

The agent randomly decided to guess.

I changed the main logic so that a confident guess would normally happen only when one valid candidate remained.

The other conditions became:

the maximum number of steps is reached;
the probabilistic override triggers.

Otherwise, the agent should continue searching.

This made the agent's normal behaviour much more consistent.

The random guess was now genuinely an exception rather than an unintended shortcut.

Debugging the Step Counter

One of the less conceptual but surprisingly persistent problems was the step counter.

At one point, the output looked something like:

Step 1
Step 3
Step 5

The agent was clearly taking actions, but the counter was being incremented incorrectly.

The issue came down to where the increment happened.

At different points, I had placed it:

at the top of the game loop;
inside individual action branches;
in logic that was also being reached elsewhere.

That meant some actions effectively caused multiple increments.

The eventual rule became simple:

One action should equal one increment.

The counter should increase exactly once, immediately before the action is recorded or displayed.

This sounds trivial, but it was a useful reminder that state variables become difficult to reason about when multiple parts of a system are allowed to modify them.

The same issue appeared again when I introduced user-play mode, which forced me to make the rule consistent across both game loops.

Adding User-Play Mode

Until this point, most testing happened against a simulated opponent.

But I also wanted to see what it would feel like for a human to actually play against the agent.

The idea was simple:

Show the available character space.
Let the user secretly choose one character.
The agent asks yes/no questions.
The user responds with y or n.
The agent updates its search space.
Eventually, it makes a guess.

This became the play_with_user() mode.

The first challenge was surprisingly practical.

Once the character space became large, printing the entire DataFrame was not useful.

Thousands of rows in a terminal did not make the game easier to understand.

So the final switch was to have the whole dataset saved in simple accessible csv.

This was a small change, but it was also the first point where I started thinking more deliberately about the difference between:

Data the program needs.

and:

Information a human actually needs to see.

Wrong Guesses and Repeated Candidates

The probabilistic guessing mechanism caused another issue in user-play mode.

When the override triggered, the agent could make a random guess.

If it was wrong, that character should obviously no longer be considered.

But initially, the logic did not fully enforce this.

The agent could potentially suggest the same rejected character again.

To fix this, I introduced a rejected set.

Every incorrect guess was stored.

Future guesses were sampled only from:

Current search space
        -
Rejected candidates

This separated two different concepts:

Remaining candidates

Characters that had not been eliminated by answers.

Rejected guesses

Characters the agent had directly tested and already proven wrong.

In the end, I also removed incorrectly guessed candidates from the active search space itself, making the game state more internally consistent.

The Random Guessing Problem

The override_probability was another feature that sounded simple but behaved differently once it was placed inside a running system.

The original idea was:

Occasionally make a guess to introduce risk and randomness.

But depending on the probability and where the check occurred, the behaviour could become too aggressive.

The agent would guess prematurely.

If the implementation was not careful, it could also repeatedly guess invalid or previously rejected candidates.

This forced me to think more carefully about what the override represented.

It was not just a random number.

It changed the entire balance between:

continuing to gather information;
taking a risk;
terminating the game early.

This would later become much more relevant when I started thinking about formal RL exploration.

At this stage, however, the solution was primarily about making the heuristic behave consistently:

trigger the override once per decision;
only guess from valid candidates;
remove rejected guesses;
otherwise continue the normal questioning strategy.
What I Was Learning From the Bugs

Looking back, these problems were more connected than they first appeared.

The binary feature issue was about deducing what had already been learned.

The feature-selection issue was about knowing when a part of the state was resolved.

The guessing issue was about defining when uncertainty was low enough to act.

The rejected-candidate issue was about maintaining a consistent state after an action failed.

And the step-counter issue was about making state transitions occur exactly once.

None of those initially felt like Reinforcement Learning problems.

They were problems of environment and agent design.

But that turned out to be important.

Before I could train an agent to learn how to make decisions, I first had to make sure I could clearly define:

what information existed;
what the agent knew;
which actions were valid;
how actions changed the state;
and when an episode should end.

In other words, I was gradually discovering the pieces of an RL environment before I had formally built one.

The Project Was Still Not RL

At this point, the agent was still heuristic-driven.

Its strategy was written by me.

I was deciding:

how features were scored;
which values were selected;
when guessing was allowed;
which questions were redundant.

There was no learned policy yet.

No reward signal.

No training loop.

No formal environment interface.

But the heuristic was becoming useful for another reason.

It gave me something to compare against.

I now had a working agent whose behaviour I could observe, benchmark, break, and eventually challenge with learned approaches.

By this point, I had:

A working sequential decision problem with a state, actions, transitions, uncertainty, and measurable outcomes.

I just hadn't formalized it as an RL environment yet.

That came next.